  "query_preview": "..\n\nDiffuse high pressure expands over the Great Lakes under zonal flow \naloft which will bring about the continuation of dry weather \novernight. The forecasted clear skies for the start of the night \nbrings minor consideration for fog overnight, however the overall \npotential remains on the lower side. Deep mixing this afternoon have \nbrought dew points down into the low to mid 30s. West boundary layer \nflow keep low-levels mixed tonight with high-based clouds then \nexpected to move in overhead as winds start to decouple. So from a \nradiational cooling perspective probabilities remain low for robust \nfog potential, but some haze closer to sunrise cannot be ruled out. \nThere is a signal for moisture advection off the southern Lake\nMichigan basin to spread inland given the west flow. Low-stratus or \nfog will likely expand across western Michigan, but there is low \nconfidence in regards the degree of expansion inland, especially \nthrough the western fringe of the cwa. Locations across or near \nHowell, Owosso, and possibly up through the southern Tri-Cities may \nsee some degree of fog and visibility restrictions pending the \nstrength of moisture advection. \n\nTomorrow, the combination of low pressure progressing across the \nOhio Valley in conjunction with an upper-level shortwave and \ntrailing cold front will likely produce light rain showers across SE \nMI. The two areas of interest will far southern Michigan where \nforcing will be predominately derived from isentropic ascent just \nahead of the progressing low, and a secondary area across across \nnorthern lower Michigan into the Tri-Cities tied to the wave and \ncold front. Some localized higher rainfall rates will be possible \nwith any frontogenetic forcing but overall no local impacts expected \ngiven a stable environment. \n\nCyclonic flow behind the departing low, reinforced from a stronger \nupper-level closed low that will retrograde across Quebec and \nOntario will usher in cooler Canadian air, will activate lake effect \nprecipitation for both Thursday and Friday. The combination of north \nto northwest flow off the lake and the boost in 0-1 km instability \nover the lake will bring about efficient moisture advection, further \nsupported by secondary vorticity lobes that will swing around the \nparent upper-level closed low. This will bring about lake effect \nrain showers on Thursday, with increasing coverage Friday as cooler \nair is advected in. As low-level flow backs NNW, the Thumb and Lake \nHuron shoreline becomes more susceptible for sustained lake effect \nactivity on Friday and Saturday. By Friday, a fully saturated dgz \nwrt to ice and freezing levels between 1-2 kft bring the possibility \nto see a rain/snow mix or melting snow flakes. Otherwise cool \nstarting Thursday and through the weekend with highs in the 40s and \nlows in the 20s to 30s. \n\nLake effect precipitation chances greatly diminish on Sunday once \nhigh pressure builds in from the west, shrinking moisture heights \nand stripping moisture from the column. High pressure to expand and \nhold into the midweek period, bringing an extended window for dry \nweather.",
